o the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the point \\((0, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\). The formulas for converting from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point \\((0, 3)\\), we have \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 3\\). Let's calculate \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\):\n\n1. Calculate \\(r\\):\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 3\\), we use the case where \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\):\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates of the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\nelif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\n\n# Convert theta to the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}