 two lines defined by the equations \\(2x = 3y = -z\\) and \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), we need to determine the direction vectors of the lines and then use the dot product formula to find the angle between them.\n\n1. **Find the direction vectors of the lines:**\n   - For the first line \\(2x = 3y = -z\\), let \\(2x = 3y = -z = t\\). Then \\(x = \\frac{t}{2}\\), \\(y = \\frac{t}{3}\\), and \\(z = -t\\). So the direction vector of the first line is \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1\\right)\\).\n   - For the second line \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), let \\(6x = -y = -4z = s\\). Then \\(x = \\frac{s}{6}\\), \\(y = -s\\), and \\(z = -\\frac{s}{4}\\). So the direction vector of the second line is \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}, -1, -\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\).\n\n2. **Normalize the direction vectors:**\n   - The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1\\right)\\) can be normalized by dividing each component by the magnitude of the vector. The magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) is \\(\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + (-1)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{9} + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9 + 4 + 36}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} = \\frac{7}{6}\\). So the normalized direction vector is \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\left(\\frac{3}{7}, \\frac{2}{7}, -\\frac{6}{7}\\right)\\).\n   - The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}, -1, -\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\) can be normalized by dividing each component by the magnitude of the vector. The magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\) is \\(\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^2 + (-1)^2 + \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} + 1 + \\frac{1}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{4 + 144 + 9}{144}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{157}{144}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{12}\\). So the normalized direction vector is \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{157}}, -\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{157}}, -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{157}}\\right)\\).\n\n3. **Find the angle between the normalized direction vectors:**