\n\\[(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 + (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 = 2 \\left( \\binom{6}{0} (\\sqrt{7})^6 + \\binom{6}{2} (\\sqrt{7})^4 (\\sqrt{5})^2 + \\binom{6}{4} (\\sqrt{7})^2 (\\sqrt{5})^4 + \\binom{6}{6} (\\sqrt{5})^6 \\right)\\]\n\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 + (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6 = 2 \\left( 7^3 + 15 \\cdot 7^2 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 + 125^3 \\right)\\]\n\\[= 2 \\left( 343 + 15 \\cdot 49 \\cdot 5 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 25 + 125 \\right)\\]\n\\[= 2 \\left( 343 + 3675 + 2625 + 125 \\right)\\]\n\\[= 2 \\left( 6768 \\right)\\]\n\\[= 13536\\]\n\nSince \\(b^6\\) is a very small number (less than 1), the greatest integer less than \\((\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6\\) is \\(13535\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the value of (sqrt(7) + sqrt(5))^6\na = math.sqrt(7) + math.sqrt(5)\nresult = a**6\n\n# Get the greatest integer less than the result\ngreatest_integer = math.floor(result)\nprint(greatest_integer)\n```\n```output\n13552\n```\nIt appears that the previous reasoning might have missed some details. Let's re-evaluate the problem step-by-step using a more precise approach.\n\nWe need to find the greatest integer less than \\((\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6\\). Let's denote \\(a = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5}\\). Note that \\(0 < b < 1\\).\n\nUsing the binomial theorem, we can expand \\(a^6\\) and \\(b^6\\):\n\n\\[a^6 = (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6\\]\n\\[b^6 = (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6\\]\n\nSince \\(b^6\\) is a very small number (because \\(0 < b < 1\\)), we can approximate \\(a^6\\) by considering the integer part of \\(a^6 + b^6\\), because \\(b^6\\) will be very close to 0 and will not affect the integer part of \\(a^6\\).\n\nFirst, let's calculate \\(a^6 + b^6\\):\n\n\\[a^6 + b^6 = (\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 + (\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{5})^6\\]\n\nUsing the binomial theorem, we can expand these terms:\n\n\\[(\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{5})^6 = \\sum_{k=