the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 7x = 8y - 53 \\]\n\nSince \\( 8y - 53 \\) must be divisible by 7, we can write:\n\n\\[ 8y \\equiv 53 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[ 8y \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\nSince \\( 8 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\), we can further simplify to:\n\n\\[ y \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\nThis means \\( y \\) can be written as \\( y = 7k + 4 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Substituting \\( y = 7k + 4 \\) back into the equation \\( 7x + 53 = 8y \\), we get:\n\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 8(7k + 4) \\]\n\\[ 7x + 53 = 56k + 32 \\]\n\\[ 7x = 56k - 21 \\]\n\\[ x = 8k - 3 \\]\n\nWe also know that the total number of coins after finding the eighth bag is more than 200:\n\n\\[ 7x + 53 > 200 \\]\n\\[ 7x > 147 \\]\n\\[ x > 21 \\]\n\nSince \\( x = 8k - 3 \\), we need \\( 8k - 3 > 21 \\):\n\n\\[ 8k > 24 \\]\n\\[ k > 3 \\]\n\nThe smallest integer value for \\( k \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( k = 4 \\). Substituting \\( k = 4 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = 8(4) - 3 = 32 - 3 = 29 \\]\n\nSo the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins is \\( 7 \\times 29 = 203 \\). Let's verify this solution:\n\n\\[ 7 \\times 29 + 53 = 203 + 53 = 256 \\]\n\\[ 256 \\div 8 = 32 \\]\n\nSince 256 is divisible by 8, the solution is correct. The smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins is \\( \\boxed{203} \\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this result.\n```python\n# We need to find the smallest x such that (7x + 53) % 8 == 0 and 7x + 53 > 200\ndef find_initial_coins():\n    k = 4  # Starting from the smallest k that satisfies k > 3\n    while True:\n        x = 8 * k - 3\n        total_coins = 7 * x + 53\n        if total_coins > 200 and total_coins % 8 == 0:\n            return 7 * x\n        k += 1\n\ninitial_coins = find_initial_coins()\nprint(initial_coins)\n```", "To solve this problem, let's denote the number of coins in each of the original seven bags as \\( x \\). Therefore, the 