HCOUNTS, so \\(M = 28\\).\n3. Let \\(S\\) be the number of students participating in the science club, so \\(S = 21\\).\n4. Let \\(N_{neither}\\) be the number of students participating in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club, so \\(N_{neither} = 6\\).\n5. Let \\(B\\) be the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club. We need to find \\(B\\).\n\nAccording to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both is \\(N - N_{neither} = 50 - 6 = 44\\). This can also be expressed as \\(M + S - B\\). So we have:\n\\[M + S - B = 44\\]\nSubstituting the values of \\(M\\) and \\(S\\), we get:\n\\[28 + 21 - B = 44\\]\nSolving for \\(B\\):\n\\[49 - B = 44\\]\n\\[B = 49 - 44\\]\n\\[B = 5\\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(5\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\nprint(both_students)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "Let's define the following:\n- \\( |M| \\) is the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( |S| \\) is the number of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |M \\cap S| \\) is the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club.\n- \\( |M \\cup S| \\) is the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both.\n- The total number of students is 50, and 6 students participate in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club. So, \\( |M \\cup S| = 50 - 6 = 44 \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |M \\cup S| = |M| + |S| - |M \\cap S| \\]\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |M \\cap S| \\]\n\\[ 44 = 49 - |M \\cap S| \\]\n\\[ |M \\cap S| = 49 - 44 \\]\n\\[ |M \\cap S| = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\r\ntotal_students = 50\r\nneither = 6\r\nmathcounts = 28\r\nscience_club = 21\r\n\r\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\r\neither_or_both = total_students - neither\r\n\r\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\r\nboth = mathcounts + science_club - either_or_both\r\nprint(both)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Let \\( A \\) be the set of students who participate in MATHCOUNTS.\n2. Let \\( B \\) be the set of students who participate in the science club.\n3. We are given:\n   - The total number of students, \\( |U| = 50 \\).\n   - The number of students who participate in MATHCOUNTS, \\( |A| = 28 \\).\n   - The number of students who participate in the science club, \\( |B| = 21 \\).\n   - The number of students who participate in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club, \\( |U - (A \\cup B)| = 6 \\).\n\n4. The number of students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both is \\( |A \\cup B| = 50 - 6 = 44 \\).\n\n5. By the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is:\n   \\[\n   |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cup B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5\n   \\]\n\nSo, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\nprint(both_students)\n```", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's denote the following:\n- \\(A\\) as the set of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\(B\\) as the set of students participating in the science club.\n- \\(n(A)\\) as the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\(n(B)\\) as the number of students participating in the science club.\n- \\(n(A \\cup B)\\) as the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both.\n- \\(n(A \\cap B)\\) as the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club.\n- \\(n(U)\\) as the total number of students in the class.\n- \\(n(U) - n(A \\cup B)\\) as the number of students participating in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(n(U) = 50\\)\n- \\(n(A) = 28\\)\n- \\(n(B) = 21\\)\n- \\(n(U) - n(A \\cup B) = 6\\)\n\nFrom the given information, we can find \\(n(A \\cup B)\\):\n\\[ n(A \\cup B) = n(U) - 6 = 50 - 6 = 44 \\]\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we know:\n\\[ n(A \\cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A \\cap B) \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - n(A \\cap B) \\]\n\nSolving for \\(n(A \\cap B)\\):\n\\[ n(A \\cap B) = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Calculate the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\n\nprint(both_students)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Let \\( A \\) be the set of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n2. Let \\( B \\) be the set of students participating in the science club.\n3. We are given:\n   - \\( |A| = 28 \\) (students in MATHCOUNTS)\n   - \\( |B| = 21 \\) (students in the science club)\n   - The total number of students is 50, and 6 students participate in neither activity. Therefore, the number of students participating in at least one of the activities is \\( 50 - 6 = 44 \\).\n4. We need to find \\( |A \\cap B| \\), the number of students participating in both activities.\n\nAccording to the principle of inclusion and exclusion:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\nWe know that \\( |A \\cup B| = 44 \\), so we can substitute the values we know:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nneither_participate = 6\nmathcounts_participants = 28\nscience_club_participants = 21\n\n# Students participating in at least one activity\nat_least_one_activity = total_students - neither_participate\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both activities\nboth_participants = mathcounts_participants + science_club_participants - at_least_one_activity\nprint(both_participants)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n- \\( |A| \\) as the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( |B| \\) as the number of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |A \\cup B| \\) as the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both.\n- \\( |A \\cap B| \\) as the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club.\n\nWe know the following:\n- The total number of students is 50.\n- The number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS is 28.\n- The number of students participating in the science club is 21.\n- The number of students participating in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club is 6.\n\nFirst, we can find the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = 50 - 6 = 44 \\]\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nstudents_mathcounts = 28\nstudents_science_club = 21\nstudents_neither = 6\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\nstudents_either_or_both = total_students - students_neither\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\nstudents_both = students_mathcounts + students_science_club - students_either_or_both\nprint(students_both)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Let \\( A \\) be the set of students who participate in MATHCOUNTS.\n2. Let \\( B \\) be the set of students who participate in the science club.\n3. We know that the total number of students is 50, and 6 students do not participate in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club. So, the number of students who participate in at least one of these activities is \\( 50 - 6 = 44 \\).\n4. We also know that \\( |A| = 28 \\) (students in MATHCOUNTS) and \\( |B| = 21 \\) (students in the science club).\n5. Let \\( |A \\cap B| \\) be the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club. According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, the number of students who participate in at least one of these activities is given by:\n   \\[\n   |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B|\n   \\]\n6. We know that \\( |A \\cup B| = 44 \\), so we can substitute the known values into the equation:\n   \\[\n   44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B|\n   \\]\n7. Solving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n   \\[\n   |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5\n   \\]\n\nSo, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nstudents_neither = 6\nstudents_mathcounts = 28\nstudents_science_club = 21\n\n# Students who participate in at least one activity\nstudents_at_least_one = total_students - students_neither\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students who participate in both\nstudents_both = students_mathcounts + students_science_club - students_at_least_one\nprint(students_both)\n```", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's denote the following:\n\n- \\( |A| \\) = number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS = 28\n- \\( |B| \\) = number of students participating in the science club = 21\n- \\( |A \\cup B| \\) = number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\n- \\( |A \\cap B| \\) = number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club (this is what we need to find)\n\nWe know that the total number of students in the class is 50, and 6 students do not participate in either activity. Therefore, the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both is \\( 50 - 6 = 44 \\). According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nPlugging in the values we know:\n\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nneither_participate = 6\nmathcounts_participants = 28\nscience_club_participants = 21\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\neither_or_both_participants = total_students - neither_participate\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nboth_participants = mathcounts_participants + science_club_participants - either_or_both_participants\nprint(both_participants)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's denote the following:\n\n- \\( |A| \\) as the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( |B| \\) as the number of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |A \\cap B| \\) as the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club.\n- \\( |A \\cup B| \\) as the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both.\n- \\( N \\) as the total number of students in the class.\n\nWe know:\n- \\( N = 50 \\)\n- \\( |A| = 28 \\)\n- \\( |B| = 21 \\)\n- The number of students who participate in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club is 6. Therefore, the number of students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both is \\( N - 6 = 50 - 6 = 44 \\). So, \\( |A \\cup B| = 44 \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\nSolving for \\( |A \\cap B| \\):\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nstudents_mathcounts = 28\nstudents_science_club = 21\nstudents_neither = 6\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\nstudents_either_or_both = total_students - students_neither\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students participating in both\nstudents_both = students_mathcounts + students_science_club - students_either_or_both\nprint(students_both)\n```", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's denote the following:\n\n- \\( A \\) as the set of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( B \\) as the set of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |A|\\) as the number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS.\n- \\( |B|\\) as the number of students participating in the science club.\n- \\( |A \\cup B|\\) as the number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both.\n- \\( |A \\cap B|\\) as the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club.\n\nFrom the problem, we know:\n- The total number of students is 50.\n- The number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS, \\( |A| = 28 \\).\n- The number of students participating in the science club, \\( |B| = 21 \\).\n- The number of students participating in neither MATHCOUNTS nor the science club is 6.\n\nThe number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both is \\( 50 - 6 = 44 \\). This is \\( |A \\cup B| \\).\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\\[ 44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\\[ 44 = 49 - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 49 - 44 \\]\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nstudents_in_mathcounts = 28\nstudents_in_science_club = 21\nstudents_in_neither = 6\n\n# Students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both\nstudents_in_either_or_both = total_students - students_in_neither\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students in both\nstudents_in_both = students_in_mathcounts + students_in_science_club - students_in_either_or_both\nprint(students_in_both)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n\n- \\( |M| \\) = number of students participating in MATHCOUNTS = 28\n- \\( |S| \\) = number of students participating in the science club = 21\n- \\( |M \\cup S| \\) = number of students participating in either MATHCOUNTS or the science club or both = 50 - 6 = 44 (since 6 students participate in neither)\n- \\( |M \\cap S| \\) = number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club (this is what we need to find)\n\nAccording to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\\[ |M \\cup S| = |M| + |S| - |