sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{((-3) - (-3 + 2\\sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28}))^2 + (2 - (10 - \\sqrt{41}))^2} = \\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28})^2 + (-8 + \\sqrt{41})^2}\n\\]\n\nFor the fourth point \\((-3 - 2\\sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28}, 10 - \\sqrt{41})\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{((-3) - (-3 - 2\\sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28}))^2 + (2 - (10 - \\sqrt{41}))^2} = \\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{-\\sqrt{41} - 28})^2 + (-8 + \\sqrt{41})^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's compute these distances and sum them up using Python.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Define the distances\r\ndistance1 = math.sqrt((2 * math.sqrt(math.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 - math.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance2 = math.sqrt((2 * math.sqrt(math.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 - math.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance3 = math.sqrt((2 * math.sqrt(-math.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 + math.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance4 = math.sqrt((2 * math.sqrt(-math.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 + math.sqrt(41))**2)\r\n\r\n# Sum of the distances\r\ntotal_distance = distance1 + distance2 + distance3 + distance4\r\nprint(total_distance)\n```\n```output\nValueError: math domain error\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the computation due to the negative values under the square roots. Let's re-evaluate the expressions and ensure we handle the complex numbers correctly.\n\nWe need to carefully handle the square roots of negative numbers, which will introduce imaginary components. However, since we are dealing with distances in the real plane, we should only consider the real parts of the distances.\n\nLet's recompute the distances, ensuring we handle the complex numbers correctly.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the distances\r\ndistance1 = cmath.sqrt((2 * cmath.sqrt(cmath.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 - cmath.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance2 = cmath.sqrt((2 * cmath.sqrt(cmath.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 - cmath.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance3 = cmath.sqrt((2 * cmath.sqrt(-cmath.sqrt(41) - 28))**2 + (-8 + cmath.sqrt(41))**2)\r\ndistance4 = cmath.sqrt