\(m\\) is \\(e\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code using sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = x / sp.ln(x)\n\n# Compute the first derivative of the function\nf_prime = sp.diff(f, x)\n\n# Solve the inequality f_prime < 0\ninequality_solution = sp.solve(f_prime < 0, x)\n\nprint(inequality_solution)\n```\n```output\n(x > 0) & (x < E) & Ne(x, 1)\n```\nThe output \\((x > 0) \\& (x < E) \\& \\text{Ne}(x, 1)\\) confirms that the function \\(f(x) = \\frac{x}{\\ln x}\\) is decreasing on the interval \\((1, e)\\). Here, \\(E\\) represents the mathematical constant \\(e\\), and \\(\\text{Ne}(x, 1)\\) means \\(x \\neq 1\\). Since we are considering the interval \\((1, m)\\), we can ignore the \\(x \\neq 1\\) part.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(m\\) for which the function is decreasing on the interval \\((1, m)\\) is \\(e\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{e}\\).\n\nLet  $f$  be a real-valued function with  $n+1$  derivatives at each point of  $\\mathbb R$ . Show that for each pair of real numbers  $a$ ,  $b$ ,  $a<b$ , such that  $$ \\ln\\left( \\frac{f(b)+f'(b)+\\cdots + f^{(n)} (b)}{f(a)+f'(a)+\\cdots + f^{(n)}(a)}\\right)=b-a $$  there is a number  $c$  in the open interval  $(a,b)$  for which  $$ f^{(n+1)}(c)=f(c) $$\nTo solve the problem, we need to use the Mean Value Theorem (MVT) and properties of derivatives. Let's break down the problem into manageable steps and then implement the solution in Python using SymPy.\n\n1. **Understand the Given Condition:**\n   The given condition is:\n   \\[\n   \\ln\\left( \\frac{f(b)+f'(b)+\\cdots + f^{(n)} (b)}{f(a)+f'(a)+\\cdots + f^{(n)}(a)}\\right)=b-a\n   \\]\n   This can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{f(b)+f'(b)+\\cdots + f^{(n)} (b)}{f(a)+f'(a)+\\cdots + f^{(n)}(a)} = e^{b-a}\n   \\]\n   Let's define:\n   \\[\n   g(x) = f(x) + f'(x) + \\cdots + f^{(n)}(x)\n   \\]\n   Then the condition becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{g(b)}{g(a)} = e^{b-a}\n 