rt the base 5 numbers to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the quotient back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s start by converting \\(413_5\\) and \\(2_5\\) to base 10.\n\nThe number \\(413_5\\) can be converted to base 10 as follows:\n\\[413_5 = 4 \\cdot 5^2 + 1 \\cdot 5^1 + 3 \\cdot 5^0 = 4 \\cdot 25 + 1 \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot 1 = 100 + 5 + 3 = 108_{10}.\\]\n\nThe number \\(2_5\\) can be converted to base 10 as follows:\n\\[2_5 = 2 \\cdot 5^0 = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2_{10}.\\]\n\nNow, we perform the division in base 10:\n\\[108_{10} \\div 2_{10} = 54_{10}.\\]\n\nNext, we need to convert the quotient \\(54_{10}\\) back to base 5. To do this, we repeatedly divide the number by 5 and keep track of the remainders.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r|l}\n54 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n10 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n0 & \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get:\n\\[54_{10} = 204_5.\\]\n\nSo the quotient of \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(204_5\\).\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to convert a base 10 number to base 5\ndef to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % 5))\n        n //= 5\n    return \'\'.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 413_5 to base 10\nnum_base_10 = 4 * 25 + 1 * 5 + 3 * 1\n\n# Convert 2_5 to base 10\ndivisor_base_10 = 2\n\n# Perform the division in base 10\nquotient_base_10 = num_base_10 // divisor_base_10\n\n# Convert the quotient back to base 5\nquotient_base_5 = to_base_5(quotient_base_10)\nprint(quotient_base_5)\n```\n```output\n204\n```\nThe quotient of \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(\\boxed{204_5}\\).', 'To solve the problem of dividing \\(413_5\\) by \\(2_5\\) and expressing the quotient in base 5, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the base 5 numbers to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the quotient back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s start by converting \\(413_5\\) and \\(2