 two bags to be the same after these moves, the ball \\(Y\\) that Bob puts into Alice's bag must be the same as the ball \\(X\\) that Alice put into Bob's bag. This is because the only way for the contents of both bags to be identical is if the ball that was removed from Alice's bag and added to Bob's bag is the same as the ball that was removed from Bob's bag and added to Alice's bag.\n\n5. **Probability Calculation**: The probability that \\(Y = X\\) is the probability that Bob selects the same ball that Alice selected. Since there are 6 balls in Bob's bag after Alice's move, the probability that Bob selects the same ball Alice selected is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\).\n\nSo, the probability that the contents of the two bags are the same after the process is \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\).\n\n### Python Code Verification\n\nLet's write Python code to simulate this process and verify the probability.\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\n# Define the colors\ncolors = ['B', 'G', 'O', 'R', 'V']\n\n# Function to simulate the process\ndef simulate():\n    # Initial bags\n    alice_bag = set(colors)\n    bob_bag = set(colors)\n    \n    # Alice's move: randomly select one ball from her bag and add it to Bob's bag\n    alice_ball = random.choice(list(alice_bag))\n    alice_bag.remove(alice_ball)\n    bob_bag.add(alice_ball)\n    \n    # Bob's move: randomly select one ball from his bag and add it to Alice's bag\n    bob_ball = random.choice(list(bob_bag))\n    bob_bag.remove(bob_ball)\n    alice_bag.add(bob_ball)\n    \n    # Check if the contents of the two bags are the same\n    return alice_bag == bob_bag\n\n# Run the simulation many times to estimate the probability\nnum_trials = 100000\nsuccess_count = 0\n\nfor _ in range(num_trials):\n    if simulate():\n        success_count += 1\n\n# Calculate the probability\nprobability = success_count / num_trials\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n1.0\n```\nThe simulation result indicates that the probability of the contents of the two 