d = i % 4\n        if i_mod == 0:\n            return f(0, j)\n        else:\n            return f(i_mod - 1, f(i_mod, j - 1))\n\n# Compute f(2015, 2)\nresult = f(2015, 2)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n4\n```\nThe value of \\( f(2015, 2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to implement the function \\( f \\) as defined and then compute \\( f(2015, 2) \\).\n\nHere's the recursive function \\( f \\) in Python:\n\n1. If \\( i = 0 \\) and \\( 0 \\le j \\le 4 \\), then \\( f(i, j) = \\text{mod}_5 (j+1) \\).\n2. If \\( i \\ge 1 \\) and \\( j = 0 \\), then \\( f(i, j) = f(i-1, 1) \\).\n3. If \\( i \\ge 1 \\) and \\( 1 \\le j \\le 4 \\), then \\( f(i, j) = f(i-1, f(i, j-1)) \\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to implement this function and then compute \\( f(2015, 2) \\).\n```python\ndef f(i, j):\n    if i == 0 and 0 <= j <= 4:\n        return (j + 1) % 5\n    elif i >= 1 and j == 0:\n        return f(i - 1, 1)\n    elif i >= 1 and 1 <= j <= 4:\n        return f(i - 1, f(i, j - 1))\n\n# Compute f(2015, 2)\nresult = f(2015, 2)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\nRecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded in comparison\n```\nThe recursion error indicates that the function is recursing too deeply, likely because the value of \\( i \\) is too large. To avoid this, we can use an iterative approach to compute the value of \\( f \\) for large \\( i \\).\n\nWe can observe that the function \\( f \\) will eventually stabilize for large \\( i \\) because the values of \\( j \\) are bounded between 0 and 4. We can precompute the values for small \\( i \\) and then use these values to compute the value for larger \\( i \\).\n\nLet's use an iterative approach to compute \\( f(2015, 2) \\).\n```python\ndef compute_f(i, j):\n    # Base cases\n    if i == 0:\n        return (j + 1) % 5\n    # Create a table to store the values of f\n    f_table = [[0] * 5 for _ in range(i + 1)]\n    # Fill the table\n    for k in range(1, i + 1):\n        f_table[k][0] = f_table[k