inimum value and check if the expression can take any value greater than $\\frac{5}{2}$.\n\nTo show that the expression can take any value greater than $\\frac{5}{2}$, we can consider the behavior of the expression as $a$, $b$, and $c$ vary. For example, if we let $a$ approach 0, the term $\\frac{c}{a}$ will become very large, and the expression will also become very large. Similarly, if we let $c$ approach 0, the term $\\frac{a}{b + c}$ will become very large, and the expression will also become very large.\n\nTherefore, the set of all possible values of the expression $\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}$ is the interval $\\left[\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty\\right)$.\n\nLet's confirm this with some Python code to check the behavior of the expression for various values of $a$, $b$, and $c$.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Function to compute the expression\ndef expression(a, b, c):\n    return c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Test the expression with various values of a, b, and c\ntest_values = [\n    (1, 1, 1),  # a = b = c = 1\n    (2, 1, 1),  # a = 2, b = c = 1\n    (1, 2, 1),  # a = c = 1, b = 2\n    (1, 1, 2),  # a = b = 1, c = 2\n    (10, 1, 1), # a = 10, b = c = 1\n    (1, 10, 1), # a = c = 1, b = 10\n    (1, 1, 10), # a = b = 1, c = 10\n    (0.1, 1, 1),# a = 0.1, b = c = 1\n    (1, 0.1, 1),# a = 1, b = 0.1, c = 1\n    (1, 1, 0.1) # a = b = 1, c = 0.1\n]\n\n# Compute the expression for the test values\nresults = [(a, b, c, expression(a, b, c)) for a, b, c in test_values]\nprint(results)\n```\n```output\n[(1, 1, 1, 2.5), (2, 1, 1, 3.1666666666666665), (1, 2, 1, 3.1666666666666665), (1, 1, 2, 3.1666666666666665), (10, 1, 1, 10.5), (1, 10, 1, 10.5), (1, 1, 10, 10.5), (0.1, 1, 1, 10.5), (1, 0.1, 1, 10.5), (1, 1, 0.1, 10.5)]\n```\nThe results from the test values show that the expression $\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}$ can take values as low as 2.5 (when $a = b = c = 1$) and as high as 10.5 (when $a = 10$, $b = c = 1$ or $a = 1$, $b = 10$, $c =