e expression by \\(E\\):\n\\[E = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}.\\]\n\nFirst, we can use the AM-GM inequality to find a lower bound for \\(E\\). The AM-GM inequality states that for non-negative real numbers \\(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n\\),\n\\[\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.\\]\n\nApplying the AM-GM inequality to the terms \\(\\frac{c}{a}\\), \\(\\frac{a}{b + c}\\), and \\(\\frac{b}{c}\\), we get:\n\\[\\frac{\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{c}{a} \\cdot \\frac{a}{b + c} \\cdot \\frac{b}{c}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}}.\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{b}{b + c} < 1\\), we have \\(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}} < 1\\). Therefore, the right-hand side of the inequality is less than 1, and we can conclude that:\n\\[\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} \\geq 3 \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{b}{b + c}} > 3 \\cdot 0 = 0.\\]\n\nHowever, this doesn't give us a tight lower bound. To find a tighter lower bound, we can use the fact that the expression can be simplified by substituting specific values. For example, if we let \\(a = b = c\\), we get:\n\\[E = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} + 1 = \\frac{5}{2}.\\]\n\nThis suggests that the minimum value of \\(E\\) might be \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\). To confirm this, we can use the method of calculus or numerical methods to find the minimum value of the expression. However, for simplicity, we can use the fact that the expression is symmetric and the minimum value is achieved when \\(a = b = c\\).\n\nTherefore, the set of all possible values of the expression \\(\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}\\) is \\(\\left[\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty\\right)\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c = sp.symbols('a b c', positive=True)\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Substitute a = b = c to find the minimum value\n