nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_and_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_and_physics = 30\n\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle\nstudents_at_least_one_subject = total_students - students_no_subject\n\n# Let P be the number of students taking physics\n# Let Ch be the number of students taking chemistry, Ch = 2P\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle formula\n# students_at_least_one_subject = students_calculus + P + 2P - students_calculus_and_physics - students_calculus_and_chemistry - students_physics_and_chemistry + students_all_three\n# 345 = 180 + P + 2P - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n# 345 = 180 + 3P - 165\n# 345 = 15 + 3P\n# 330 = 3P\n# P = 110\n\nP = (students_at_least_one_subject - students_calculus + students_calculus_and_physics + students_calculus_and_chemistry + students_physics_and_chemistry - students_all_three) // 3\nprint(P)\n```", "Let's denote the number of students taking physics as \\(P\\), chemistry as \\(C\\), and calculus as \\(K\\). We are given the following information:\n\n1. The total number of students is 360.\n2. 15 students take all three subjects.\n3. 15 students take none of the subjects.\n4. 180 students take calculus.\n5. Twice as many students take chemistry as take physics, so \\(C = 2P\\).\n6. 75 students take both calculus and chemistry.\n7. 75 students take both physics and chemistry.\n8. 30 students take both physics and calculus.\n\nWe can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students taking physics. Let's denote the number of students taking exactly two subjects as follows:\n- \\(a\\) students take calculus and physics but not chemistry.\n- \\(b\\) students take calculus and chemistry but not physics.\n- \\(c\\) students take physics and chemistry but not calculus.\n\nFrom the given information, we have:\n- \\(a + 15 + b = 180\\) (students taking calculus).\n- \\(b + 15 + c = 75\\) (students taking both calculus and chemistry).\n- \\(c + 15 + a = 75\\) (students taking both physics and chemistry).\n- \\(b + 15 = 75\\) (students taking both physics and chemistry).\n- \\(a + 15 = 30\\) (students taking both physics and calculus).\n\nWe can solve these equations step by step. First, let's solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[a + 15 = 30 \\implies a = 15\\]\n\\[b + 15 = 75 \\implies b = 60\\]\n\\[c + 15 + a = 75 \\implies c + 15 + 15 = 75 \\implies c = 45\\]\n\nNow we can find the total number of students taking physics:\n\\[P = a + c + 15 + \\text{(students taking only physics)}\\]\nThe number of students taking only physics is \\(P - (a + c + 15)\\). Since \\(C = 2P\\), we can use the inclusion-exclusion principle to find \\(P\\):\n\\[P + C + K - (a + b + c + 15) - \\text{(students taking none)} = 360 - 15\\]\n\\[P + 2P + 180 - (15 + 60 + 45 + 15) - 15 = 345\\]\n\\[3P + 180 - 135 = 345\\]\n\\[3P + 45 = 345\\]\n\\[3P = 300\\]\n\\[P = 100\\]\n\nSo the number of students taking physics is \\(100\\). Let's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our solution.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_no_subject = 15\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_chemistry = 2 * students_physics\nstudents_calculus_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_physics = 30\n\n# Let's solve for the number of students taking only calculus, only chemistry, and only physics\nstudents_calculus_only = students_calculus - students_calculus_chemistry - students_calculus_physics + students_all_three\nstudents_chemistry_only = students_chemistry - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\nstudents_physics_only = students_physics - students_calculus_physics - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle to find the number of students taking physics\nstudents_physics = students_physics_only + students_calculus_physics + students_physics_chemistry - students_all_three + students_physics\n\n# We know that students_chemistry = 2 * students_physics\n# And we can use the total number of students to verify our solution\nstudents_total = students_calculus + students_chemistry + students_physics - (students_calculus_chemistry + students_physics_chemistry + students_calculus_physics - students_all_three) - students_no_subject\n\n# Let's solve for students_physics\nstudents_physics = (students_total - students_calculus - students_chemistry + students_all_three + students_no_subject) / 3\n\nprint(students_physics)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'students_physics' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the variable names in the code. Let's correct the code and solve for the number of students taking physics again. We'll use the inclusion-exclusion principle and the given constraints to find the value of \\(P\\).\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_no_subject = 15\nstudents_all_three = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_chemistry = 2 * 100  # We will verify this later\nstudents_calculus_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_physics = 30\n\n# Using the inclusion-exclusion principle to find the number of students taking physics\n# Let's denote the number of students taking only calculus, only chemistry, and only physics as follows:\n# students_calculus_only = students_calculus - students_calculus_chemistry - students_calculus_physics + students_all_three\n# students_chemistry_only = students_chemistry - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n# students_physics_only = students_physics - students_calculus_physics - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n\n# We know that students_chemistry = 2 * students_physics\n# And we can use the total number of students to verify our solution\nstudents_total = students_calculus + students_chemistry + students_physics - (students_calculus_chemistry + students_physics_chemistry + students_calculus_physics - students_all_three) - students_no_subject\n\n# Let's solve for students_physics\nstudents_physics = (students_total - students_calculus - students_chemistry + students_all_three + students_no_subject) / 3\n\nprint(students_physics)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'students_physics' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there is still an issue with the variable names. Let's simplify the approach and solve for the number of students taking physics directly using the given constraints and the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nWe know:\n1. \\(C = 2P\\)\n2. \\(K = 180\\)\n3. \\(C \\cap K = 75\\)\n4. \\(P \\cap K = 30\\)\n5. \\(P \\cap C = 75\\)\n6. \\(P \\cap C \\cap K = 15\\)\n7. \\(N = 360\\)\n8. \\(N_{\\text{none}} = 15\\)\n\nUsing the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\\[N = K + C + P - (C \\cap K + P \\cap K + P \\cap C) + P \\cap C \\cap K + N_{\\text{none}}\\]\n\\[360 = 180 + 2P + P - (75 + 30 + 75) + 15 + 15\\]\n\\[360 = 180 + 3P - 180 + 30\\]\n\\[360 = 3P + 30\\]\n\\[330 = 3P\\]\n\\[P = 110\\]\n\nSo the number of students taking physics is \\(110\\). Let's verify this with the final answer.\n\\[\n\\boxed{100}\n\\]", "Let's solve the problem step by step. We'll use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students taking physics.\n\n1. Let's denote the number of students taking physics as \\( P \\), the number of students taking calculus as \\( C \\), and the number of students taking chemistry as \\( Ch \\).\n2. We are given the following information:\n   - Total number of students, \\( N = 360 \\).\n   - Number of students taking calculus, \\( C = 180 \\).\n   - Number of students taking both calculus and chemistry, \\( C \\cap Ch = 75 \\).\n   - Number of students taking both physics and chemistry, \\( P \\cap Ch = 75 \\).\n   - Number of students taking both physics and calculus, \\( P \\cap C = 30 \\).\n   - Number of students taking all three subjects, \\( C \\cap P \\cap Ch = 15 \\).\n   - Number of students taking none of the subjects, \\( N - (C \\cup P \\cup Ch) = 15 \\).\n3. Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion for three sets, we have:\n   \\[\n   |C \\cup P \\cup Ch| = |C| + |P| + |Ch| - |C \\cap P| - |C \\cap Ch| - |P \\cap Ch| + |C \\cap P \\cap Ch|\n   \\]\n   Since 15 students don't take any of the subjects, the number of students taking at least one of the subjects is \\( 360 - 15 = 345 \\). So we have:\n   \\[\n   345 = 180 + P + Ch - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n   \\[\n   345 = 180 + P + Ch - 165\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   345 = 15 + P + Ch\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P + Ch = 330\n   \\]\n4. We are also given that the number of students taking chemistry is twice the number of students taking physics, so \\( Ch = 2P \\). Substituting \\( Ch = 2P \\) into the equation \\( P + Ch = 330 \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   P + 2P = 330\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3P = 330\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P = 110\n   \\]\n5. Therefore, the number of students taking physics is \\( 110 \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 360\nstudents_no_subject = 15\nstudents_calculus = 180\nstudents_calculus_chemistry = 75\nstudents_physics_chemistry = 75\nstudents_calculus_physics = 30\nstudents_all_three = 15\n\n# Number of students taking at least one subject\nstudents_at_least_one_subject = total_students - students_no_subject\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion\n# students_at_least_one_subject = students_calculus + students_physics + students_chemistry - students_calculus_physics - students_calculus_chemistry - students_physics_chemistry + students_all_three\n# 345 = 180 + students_physics + students_chemistry - 30 - 75 - 75 + 15\n# 345 = 180 + students_physics + students_chemistry - 165\n# 345 = 15 + students_physics + students_chemistry\n# students_physics + students_chemistry = 330\n\n# Given that students_chemistry = 2 * students_physics\nstudents_physics = 330 / 3\nstu