ther.\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other.\n\nSince the boxes are indistinguishable, we do not need to count permutations of the same distribution (e.g., 4 balls in the first box and 1 in the second is the same as 4 balls in the second box and 1 in the first).\n\nSo, there are only 3 distinct ways to distribute the balls. We can confirm this by writing a Python script to enumerate all possible distributions and count the unique ones.\n\nHere's the Python code to do that:\n```python\n# Function to count the number of ways to distribute n indistinguishable balls into k indistinguishable boxes\ndef count_ways(n, k):\n    ways = set()\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        j = n - i\n        # Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we only need to consider (i, j) where i <= j\n        if i <= j:\n            ways.add((i, j))\n    return len(ways)\n\n# Number of balls and boxes\nn = 5\nk = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of ways\nnumber_of_ways = count_ways(n, k)\nprint(number_of_ways)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to consider the fact that the balls are indistinguishable and the boxes are also indistinguishable. This means that we need to find the number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes.\n\nThe problem can be approached by considering the possible distributions of the balls into the boxes. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we only need to consider the number of balls in each box, and the order of the boxes does not matter. The possible distributions are as follows:\n\n1. All 5 balls in one box and 0 in the other.\n2. 4 balls in one box and 1 in the other.\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other.\n\nSince the boxes are indistinguishable, the distribution (4 balls in one box and 1 in the other) is the same as (1 ball in one box and 4 in the other), and similarly for the other distributions