e distributions\nunique_distributions = 0\nfor i in range(balls + 1):\n    if i <= balls // 2:\n        unique_distributions += 1\n\nprint(unique_distributions)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem of determining the number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes, we need to consider the possible distributions of the balls. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, the order of the boxes does not matter. The possible distributions are as follows:\n\n1. All 5 balls in one box and 0 in the other.\n2. 4 balls in one box and 1 in the other.\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other.\n4. 2 balls in one box and 3 in the other (this is the same as the previous case since the boxes are indistinguishable).\n5. 1 ball in one box and 4 in the other (this is the same as the second case).\n6. 0 balls in one box and 5 in the other (this is the same as the first case).\n\nWe can see that there are only 3 unique distributions: (5,0), (4,1), and (3,2). Therefore, there are 3 ways to distribute the 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes.\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code snippet using sympy to illustrate the reasoning:\n\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the number of balls and boxes\nballs = 5\nboxes = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of ways to distribute the balls into the boxes\n# Since the boxes are indistinguishable, we only need to consider unique distributions\n# The possible distributions are (5,0), (4,1), and (3,2)\nways = 0\n\n# Iterate through possible distributions\nfor i in range(balls + 1):\n    if i <= balls - i:\n        ways += 1\n\nprint(ways)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to distribute 5 indistinguishable balls in