tributions of 5 balls into 2 boxes are as follows:\n\n1. All 5 balls in one box and 0 in the other.\n2. 4 balls in one box and 1 in the other.\n3. 3 balls in one box and 2 in the other.\n\nSince the boxes are indistinguishable, we do not need to count the cases where the balls are distributed differently in the boxes (e.g., 4 balls in the first box and 1 in the second is the same as 4 balls in the second box and 1 in the first).\n\nTherefore, there are exactly 3 distinct ways to distribute the balls. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n\nWe can use a simple loop to iterate through the possible distributions and count the unique ones. Here's the code to do that:\n\n```python\n# Function to count the number of ways to distribute n indistinguishable balls into k indistinguishable boxes\ndef count_ways(n, k):\n    ways = 0\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        if i <= n and (n - i) <= n:\n            ways += 1\n    return ways // 2 if k > 1 else ways\n\n# Number of balls and boxes\nn = 5\nk = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of ways\nresult = count_ways(n, k)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem of determining how many ways there are to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes, we need to consider the partitions of the number 5. Each partition represents a way to distribute the balls into the boxes. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Identify all possible ways to partition the number 5 into up to 2 parts.\n2. Since the boxes are indistinguishable, the order of the parts in the partition does not matter.\n\nThe partitions of 5 into up to 2 parts are:\n- 5 + 0\n- 4 + 1\n- 3 + 2\n- 2 + 3 (This is the same as 3 + 2)\n- 1 + 4 (This is the same as 4 + 1)\n- 0 + 5 (This is the same as 5 + 0)\n\nSo, the distinct partitions are 5 + 0, 4 + 1, and 3 + 2. Therefore, there are 3 distinct ways to distribute the balls into the boxes.\n\nLet's write 