{v}$, respectively.\n\nFirst, we need to find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$. These vectors can be computed as follows:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} = A - B = (1 - 3, -11 - (-4), 2 - 1) = (-2, -7, 1)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BC} = C - B = (-2 - 3, 1 - (-4), -1 - 1) = (-5, 5, -2)\n\\]\n\nNext, we compute the dot product of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{BC} = (-2)(-5) + (-7)(5) + (1)(-2) = 10 - 35 - 2 = -27\n\\]\n\nThen, we compute the magnitudes of $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{BC}$:\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{BA}\\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-7)^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 49 + 1} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{BC}\\| = \\sqrt{(-5)^2 + 5^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 25 + 4} = \\sqrt{54} = 3\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nNow we can find $\\cos \\theta$:\n\\[\n\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\overrightarrow{BA} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{BC}}{\\|\\overrightarrow{BA}\\| \\|\\overrightarrow{BC}\\|} = \\frac{-27}{(3\\sqrt{6})(3\\sqrt{6})} = \\frac{-27}{54} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, $\\theta = \\cos^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$. The angle whose cosine is $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is $120^\\circ$. Therefore, $\\angle ABC = 120^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define points A, B, and C\nA = np.array([1, -11, 2])\nB = np.array([3, -4, 1])\nC = np.array([-2, 1, -1])\n\n# Compute vectors BA and BC\nBA = A - B\nBC = C - B\n\n# Compute the dot product of BA and BC\ndot_product = np.dot(BA, BC)\n\n# Compute the magnitudes of BA and BC\nmagnitude_BA = np.linalg.norm(BA)\nmagnitude_BC = np.linalg.norm(BC)\n\n# Compute the cosine of the angle\ncos_theta = dot_product / (magnitude_BA * magnitude_BC)\n\n# Compute the angle in radians and then convert to degrees\nangle_radians = np.arccos(cos_theta)\nangle_degrees = np.degrees(angle_radians)\n\nprint(angle_degrees)\n```\n```output\n120.00000000000001\n```\nThe computed angle $\\angle A