nd $N$ respectively.\n\n2. **Angle Properties**: The angle between a tangent and a radius at the point of tangency is 90 degrees. So, $\\angle HOJ = 90^\\circ$ and $\\angle HNJ = 90^\\circ$.\n\n3. **Sum of Angles**: Since $HO$ and $HN$ are tangents, the angles $\\angle HOJ$ and $\\angle HNJ$ are both right angles (90 degrees). The sum of these angles is $90^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ$. However, we need to find the sum of $m\\angle J$ and $m\\angle H$.\n\n4. **Central Angle and Inscribed Angle**: The angle $\\angle J$ (which is the central angle subtended by the arc $ON$) is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the circumference. Since $HO$ and $HN$ are tangents, the angle $\\angle H$ subtended by the arc $ON$ at the point $H$ (outside the circle) is half of the central angle $\\angle J$. Therefore, $\\angle J = 2 \\cdot \\angle H$.\n\n5. **Sum of Angles in Quadrilateral**: The sum of the angles in quadrilateral $HONJ$ is $360^\\circ$. We know that $\\angle HOJ = 90^\\circ$ and $\\angle HNJ = 90^\\circ$. So, the sum of the remaining angles $\\angle J$ and $\\angle H$ is $360^\\circ - 90^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ$. Since $\\angle J = 2 \\cdot \\angle H$, we can set up the equation $\\angle J + \\angle H = 180^\\circ$ and $\\angle J = 2 \\cdot \\angle H$. Solving these equations, we get $\\angle H = 60^\\circ$ and $\\angle J = 120^\\circ$. Therefore, the sum of $\\angle J$ and $\\angle H$ is $120^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nangle_H, angle_J = sp.symbols('angle_H angle_J')\n\n# Set up the equations\neq1 = sp.Eq(angle_J + angle_H, 180)\neq2 = sp.Eq(angle_J, 2 * angle_H)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = sp.solve((eq1, eq2), (angle_H, angle_J))\nangle_H_value = solution[angle_H]\nangle_J_value = solution[angle_J]\n\n# Calculate the sum of the angles\nsum_of_angles = angle_H_value + angle_J_value\nprint(sum_of_angles)\n```\n```ou