 verify these calculations.\n```python\n# Define the full price of one ticket\nfull_price = 20\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Susan\ndiscount_susan = 0.25\ntickets_susan = 4\ndiscounted_price_susan = full_price * (1 - discount_susan)\ntotal_cost_susan = discounted_price_susan * tickets_susan\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Pam\ndiscount_pam = 0.30\ntickets_pam = 5\ndiscounted_price_pam = full_price * (1 - discount_pam)\ntotal_cost_pam = discounted_price_pam * tickets_pam\n\n# Calculate the difference\ndifference = total_cost_pam - total_cost_susan\nprint(difference)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe difference in the amount paid by Pam and Susan is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) dollars.", "First, let's calculate the total cost for Susan and Pam after applying their respective discounts.\n\nSusan buys 4 tickets with a 25% discount:\n- The full price for 4 tickets is \\(4 \\times 20 = 80\\) dollars.\n- The discount is \\(25\\%\\) of 80 dollars, which is \\(0.25 \\times 80 = 20\\) dollars.\n- So, the total cost for Susan is \\(80 - 20 = 60\\) dollars.\n\nPam buys 5 tickets with a 30% discount:\n- The full price for 5 tickets is \\(5 \\times 20 = 100\\) dollars.\n- The discount is \\(30\\%\\) of 100 dollars, which is \\(0.30 \\times 100 = 30\\) dollars.\n- So, the total cost for Pam is \\(100 - 30 = 70\\) dollars.\n\nNow, we need to find out how many more dollars Pam pays than Susan:\n\\[ 70 - 60 = 10 \\]\n\nSo, Pam pays 10 dollars more than Susan.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the full price of one ticket\nticket_price = 20\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Susan\nsusan_tickets = 4\nsusan_discount = 0.25\nsusan_total_cost = susan_tickets * ticket_price * (1 - susan_discount)\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Pam\npam_tickets = 5\npam_discount = 0.30\npam_total_cost = pam_tickets * ticket_price * (1 - pam_discount)\n\n# Calculate the difference in cost\ncost_difference = pam_total_cost - susan_total_cost\nprint(cost_difference)\n```\n```output\n10.