ount is \\(20 - 5 = 15\\) dollars.\n   - The total cost for Susan is \\(4 \\times 15 = 60\\) dollars.\n\n2. Calculate the total cost for Pam:\n   - Pam buys 5 tickets with a 30% discount.\n   - The discount amount per ticket is \\(20 \\times 0.30 = 6\\) dollars.\n   - The price per ticket after discount is \\(20 - 6 = 14\\) dollars.\n   - The total cost for Pam is \\(5 \\times 14 = 70\\) dollars.\n\n3. Find the difference in the amounts paid by Pam and Susan:\n   - The difference is \\(70 - 60 = 10\\) dollars.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the original price of one ticket\noriginal_price = 20\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Susan\nsusan_discount = 0.25\nsusan_tickets = 4\nsusan_total_cost = susan_tickets * (original_price * (1 - susan_discount))\n\n# Calculate the total cost for Pam\npam_discount = 0.30\npam_tickets = 5\npam_total_cost = pam_tickets * (original_price * (1 - pam_discount))\n\n# Calculate the difference in the amounts paid by Pam and Susan\ndifference = pam_total_cost - susan_total_cost\nprint(difference)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe difference in the amounts paid by Pam and Susan is \\(\\boxed{10}\\) dollars.", "To find the modular inverse of 997 modulo 1000, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 997x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000} \\]\n\nThis is equivalent to finding \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 997x = 1000k + 1 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\( k \\). This can be solved using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which finds solutions to equations of the form:\n\n\\[ ax + by = \\gcd(a, b) \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 997 \\) and \\( b = 1000 \\). Since 997 and 1000 are coprime (i.e., \\(\\gcd(997, 1000) = 1\\)), there exist integers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 997x + 1000y = 1 \\]\n\nThe value of \\( x \\) from this equation will be the modular inverse of 997 modulo 1000. Let's use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find \\( x \\).\n\nWe start by performing the Euclidean Algorithm to find the gcd:\n\n1. \\( 