tarting from 3\nfor a_val in range(3, 100):  # We can limit the range for practical purposes\n    # Find all possible sets of roots (r1, r2, r3) that sum to a\n    roots = []\n    for r1 in range(1, a_val):\n        for r2 in range(1, a_val - r1 + 1):\n            r3 = a_val - r1 - r2\n            if r3 > 0 and r1 * r2 + r2 * r3 + r3 * r1 == (a_val**2 - 81) / 2:\n                roots.append((r1, r2, r3))\n    \n    # If there are more than one set of roots, calculate possible values of c\n    if len(roots) > 1:\n        for root_set in roots:\n            r1, r2, r3 = root_set\n            c_val = 2 * r1 * r2 * r3\n            possible_values_of_c.add(c_val)\n\n# Sum the possible values of c\nsum_of_possible_values_of_c = sum(possible_values_of_c)\nprint(sum_of_possible_values_of_c)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems that the code did not find any valid sets of roots for the polynomial, leading to an empty set of possible values for \\(c\\). Let's re-evaluate the approach and constraints.\n\nWe need to ensure that the polynomial \\(P(x) = 2x^3 - 2ax^2 + (a^2 - 81)x - c\\) has roots that are positive integers. The roots \\(r_1, r_2, r_3\\) must satisfy:\n1. \\(r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = a\\)\n2. \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}\\)\n3. \\(r_1r_2r_3 = \\frac{c}{2}\\)\n\nWe need to check for values of \\(a\\) and find the corresponding sets of roots. Let's re-implement the code with a more detailed check for the roots and ensure we correctly calculate \\(c\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n\n# Define the polynomial and its roots\na = sp.symbols('a', integer=True)\nc = sp.symbols('c', integer=True)\n\n# We need to find the possible values of a and c\npossible_values_of_c = set()\n\n# We will check for values of a starting from 3\nfor a_val in range(3, 100):  # We can limit the range for practical purposes\n    # Find all possible sets of roots (r1, r2, r3) that sum to a\n    roots = []\n    for r1 in range(