qrt{i - 1}\\) and \\(-i - \\sqrt{i - 1}\\) is the real part of \\(\\sqrt{i - 1}\\), and the distance between \\(-i + i\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(-i - i\\sqrt{2}\\) is the imaginary part of \\(i\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nLet's calculate the area of the rectangle. The length of the horizontal side is \\(2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\sqrt{2}\\) and the length of the vertical side is \\(2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\sqrt{2}\\). So the area of the rectangle is \\(\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 2\\).\n\nThe area of the polygon is \\(2 = 2^{1/1}\\), so \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), and \\(p = 2\\). Therefore, \\(a + b + p = 1 + 1 + 2 = 4\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial coefficients\r\ncoefficients = [1, 4j, -6, -4j, -1]\r\n\r\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\r\nroots = np.roots(coefficients)\r\n\r\n# Extract the roots\r\nroot1 = roots[0]\r\nroot2 = roots[1]\r\nroot3 = roots[2]\r\nroot4 = roots[3]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the area of the rectangle formed by the roots\r\n# The roots are symmetric, so we can calculate the distance between the real parts and imaginary parts\r\nhorizontal_distance = abs(root1.real - root2.real)\r\nvertical_distance = abs(root3.imag - root4.imag)\r\n\r\n# The area of the rectangle\r\narea = horizontal_distance * vertical_distance\r\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n3.0000000000000004\n```\nThe area of the rectangle formed by the roots of the polynomial is \\(3\\). This can be expressed as \\(3^1\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), and \\(p = 3\\). Therefore, \\(a + b + p = 1 + 1 + 3 = 5\\).\n\nSo the final answer is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the roots of the polynomial equation \\(z^4 + 4z^3i - 6z^2 - 4zi - i = 0\\). These roots are the vertices of a convex polygon in the complex plane. We will then find the area of this polygon.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the polynomial. We can use Python to find the roots accurately. Once we have the roots, we can deter