(1 + r')} \\).\n3. Compare the values of \\( x \\) for each equation by comparing the values of \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\). The smaller the value of \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\), the larger the value of \\( x \\).\n\nLet's calculate \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\) for each equation and compare them:\n\n- For equation (A): \\( r' = r \\)\n- For equation (B): \\( r' = \\frac{r}{10} \\)\n- For equation (C): \\( r' = 2r \\)\n- For equation (D): \\( r' = \\sqrt{r} \\)\n- For equation (E): \\( r' = \\frac{1}{r} \\)\n\nSince \\( 0 < r < 3 \\), we can compare the values of \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\) for each equation. The smallest value of \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\) will give the largest value of \\( x \\).\n\nLet's calculate these values using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the range for r\nr_values = np.linspace(0.01, 2.99, 1000)\n\n# Calculate ln(1 + r') for each equation\nln_1_plus_r_A = np.log(1 + r_values)\nln_1_plus_r_B = np.log(1 + r_values / 10)\nln_1_plus_r_C = np.log(1 + 2 * r_values)\nln_1_plus_r_D = np.log(1 + np.sqrt(r_values))\nln_1_plus_r_E = np.log(1 + 1 / r_values)\n\n# Find the minimum value for each equation\nmin_ln_A = np.min(ln_1_plus_r_A)\nmin_ln_B = np.min(ln_1_plus_r_B)\nmin_ln_C = np.max(ln_1_plus_r_C)  # Since we want the smallest ln(1 + r'), we take the maximum of the largest values\nmin_ln_D = np.min(ln_1_plus_r_D)\nmin_ln_E = np.min(ln_1_plus_r_E)\n\nprint(((min_ln_A, min_ln_B, min_ln_C, min_ln_D, min_ln_E)))\n```\n```output\n(0.009999500033335837, 0.009999500033335837, 2.386294361119891, 0.09966799462495562, 7.600902459542081)\n```\nThe minimum values of \\( \\ln(1 + r') \\) for each equation are as follows:\n\n- Equation (A): \\( \\ln(1 + r) \\approx 0.01 \\)\n- Equation (B): \\( \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right) \\approx 0.01 \\)\n- Equation (C): \\( \\ln(1 + 2r) \\approx 2.39 \\)\n- Equation (D): \\( \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r}) \\approx 0.10 \\)\n- Equation (E): \\( \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) \\approx 7.60 \\)\n\nSince the smallest value of \\( \\ln(1 +