 (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to break this down into simpler parts. The cross product distributes over vector addition, so we can write:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this expression by noting that the cross product of a vector with itself is zero, i.e., \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\). Also, the cross product is anti-commutative, so \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\). Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute this step by step. First, we compute \\(6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Next, we compute \\(-2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Finally, we compute \\(-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the scalar multiplication property of the cross product.\n\nThe cross product distributes over vector addition and is linear in each argument. Therefore, we can expand the expression as follows:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we can simplify this expression:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\text{(since the cross product of a vector with itself is zero)}.\n\\]\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mat