 substitute \\(x = a\\):\n\n\\[p(a) = \\frac{(a - b)(a - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(a - a)(a - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1.\\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\(x = b\\):\n\n\\[p(b) = \\frac{(b - b)(b - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(b - a)(b - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\\]\n\nFinally, let's substitute \\(x = c\\):\n\n\\[p(c) = \\frac{(c - b)(c - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(c - a)(c - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\\]\n\nFrom these substitutions, we see that \\(p(x) = 1\\) for \\(x = a\\), \\(x = b\\), and \\(x = c\\). Since \\(p(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree at most 2, and it takes the value 1 at three distinct points \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), it must be the constant polynomial \\(p(x) = 1\\).\n\nTherefore, the degree of the polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is 0. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\r\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the polynomial\r\np_x_simplified = simplify(p_x)\r\nprint(p_x_simplified)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is \\(1\\), which is a constant polynomial. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "To determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), let's first simplify the expression. We can start by examining the polynomial:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nWe can use a common technique to simplify this expression by recognizing that the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is a Lagrange polynomial of degree 2. The Lagrange polynomial is a way to interpolate a function through a 