 code.\n```python\n# Define the solutions\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the solutions\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation $|x-4| - 10 = 2$, we first isolate the absolute value expression. Adding 10 to both sides of the equation, we get:\n\\[ |x-4| = 12. \\]\nThis equation means that $x-4$ is either 12 or -12. So we have two cases to consider:\n1. $x-4 = 12$\n2. $x-4 = -12$\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n1. For $x-4 = 12$, we add 4 to both sides:\n\\[ x = 16. \\]\n2. For $x-4 = -12$, we add 4 to both sides:\n\\[ x = -8. \\]\n\nSo the possible values of $x$ are 16 and -8. The product of these values is:\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128. \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the possible values of x\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the possible values of x\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation \\( |x-4| - 10 = 2 \\), we first isolate the absolute value term:\n\n\\[ |x-4| = 12 \\]\n\nThe equation \\( |x-4| = 12 \\) means that \\( x-4 \\) can be either 12 or -12. Therefore, we have two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\nWe solve each case separately:\n\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n\\[ x = 12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = 16 \\]\n\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\\[ x = -12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = -8 \\]\n\nSo, the possible values of \\( x \\) are \\( 16 \\) and \\( -8 \\). The product of these values is:\n\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128 \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the possible values of x\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the possible values of x\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the e