e separately:\n\n1. $x-4 = 12$\n\\[ x = 12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = 16 \\]\n\n2. $x-4 = -12$\n\\[ x = -12 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x = -8 \\]\n\nSo the two possible values of $x$ are 16 and -8. The product of these values is:\n\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the two possible values of x\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the two values\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation \\(|x-4| - 10 = 2\\), we first isolate the absolute value term:\n\n\\[|x-4| = 12\\]\n\nThis absolute value equation can be split into two separate linear equations:\n\n\\[x-4 = 12 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x-4 = -12\\]\n\nWe solve each equation separately:\n\n1. For \\(x-4 = 12\\):\n\\[x = 12 + 4\\]\n\\[x = 16\\]\n\n2. For \\(x-4 = -12\\):\n\\[x = -12 + 4\\]\n\\[x = -8\\]\n\nThe two solutions are \\(x = 16\\) and \\(x = -8\\). The product of these solutions is:\n\n\\[16 \\times (-8) = -128\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the solutions\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of the solutions\nproduct = x1 * x2\nprint(product)\n```\n```output\n-128\n```\nThe product of all possible values of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\).", "To solve the equation \\(|x-4| - 10 = 2\\), we first isolate the absolute value expression:\n\n\\[|x-4| = 12.\\]\n\nThe absolute value equation \\(|x-4| = 12\\) means that the expression inside the absolute value can be either 12 or -12. So we have two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\(x-4 = 12\\)\n2. \\(x-4 = -12\\)\n\nWe solve each case separately:\n\n1. \\(x-4 = 12 \\implies x = 16\\)\n2. \\(x-4 = -12 \\implies x = -8\\)\n\nSo the solutions to the equation are \\(x = 16\\) and \\(x = -8\\). The product of these solutions is:\n\n\\[16 \\cdot (-8) = -128.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the solutions\nx1 = 16\nx2 = -8\n\n# Calculate the product of 