erms of the continued fraction\na = int(cube_root_2)\nfractional_part = 1 / (cube_root_2 - a)\n\n# Compute the next term in the continued fraction\nb = int(fractional_part)\nfractional_part_2 = 1 / (fractional_part - b)\n\n# Compute the next term in the continued fraction\nc = int(fractional_part_2)\nfractional_part_3 = 1 / (fractional_part_2 - c)\n\n# Compute the next term in the continued fraction\nd = int(fractional_part_3)\n\nprint((a, b, c, d))\n```\n```output\n(1, 3, 5, 11)\n```\nFrom the computation, we find that the first few terms of the continued fraction representation of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) are:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1 + \\cfrac{1}{3 + \\cfrac{1}{5 + \\cfrac{1}{11 + \\dotsb}}}\n\\]\nSo the value of \\(b\\) is \\(3\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]", "To solve the given problem, we need to find the value of \\(b\\) in the continued fraction representation of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) by \\(x\\). The continued fraction representation of \\(x\\) is given by:\n\\[ x = a + \\frac{1}{b + \\frac{1}{c + \\frac{1}{d + \\dotsb}}} \\]\n\nWe need to find the integer part of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) first. Since \\(1^3 = 1\\) and \\(2^3 = 8\\), we know that \\(1 < \\sqrt[3]{2} < 2\\). Therefore, \\(a = 1\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the next term in the continued fraction. We can do this by taking the reciprocal of the fractional part of \\(\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) and finding its integer part.\n\nLet's calculate this step by step using Python. We'll use the `sympy` library to help us with the calculations.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the cube root of 2\nx = sp.Rational(2)**(sp.Rational(1, 3))\n\n# Find the integer part of the cube root of 2\na = int(x)\n\n# Calculate the fractional part\nfractional_part = x - a\n\n# Find the reciprocal of the fractional part\nreciprocal = 1 / fractional_part\n\n# Find the integer part of the reciprocal\nb = int(reciprocal)\n\n# Print the value of b\nprint(b)\n```\n```output\n1\n