boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed', "Find the units digit of $18^5.$\n/hint\nTo find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. So, we need to find the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nSimilarly, to find the units digit of \\(18^5\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. So, we need to find the units digit of \\(8^5\\).\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Find the units digit of \\(8^1\\).\n2. Find the units digit of \\(8^2\\).\n3. Find the units digit of \\(8^3\\).\n4. Find the units digit of \\(8^4\\).\n5. Find the units digit of \\(8^5\\).\n6. Find the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nWe can observe the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8. Let's calculate these values using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of 8^n\ndef units_digit_of_power(base, exponent):\n    # We only need the units digit of the base\n    base_units_digit = base % 10\n    # Calculate the units digit of the power\n    units_digit = (base_units_digit ** exponent) % 10\n    return units_digit\n\n# Find the units digit of 8^6\nunits_digit_8_6 = units_digit_of_power(8, 6)\n# Find the units digit of 8^5\nunits_digit_8_5 = units_digit_of_power(8, 5)\n\nprint((units_digit_8_6, units_digit_8_5))\n```\n```output\n(4, 2)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^6\\), which is 4. The units digit of \\(18^5\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^5\\), which is 2.\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4} \\quad \\text{(units digit of } 18^6 \\text{)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{2} \\quad \\text{(units digit of } 18^5 \\text{)}\n\\]", "To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. The units digit of \\(18^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nWe can determine the units digit of \\(8^6\\) by observing the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n8^1 & = 8 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^2 & = 64 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n8^3 & = 512 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 2) \\\\\n8^4 & = 4096 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 6) \\\\\n8^5 & = 32768 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFrom the above calculations, we can see that the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is 4. Therefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is also 4.\n\nLet's confirm this by using Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the units digit of 18^6\nunits_digit = (18 ** 6) % 10\nprint(units_digit)\n```\n```output\n4\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", 'Find the units digit of $12^{12}.$ \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxe