ie within the bounds \\([-1, 1]\\) for all \\(x\\) in the interval \\([0, 1]\\).\n\nWe will evaluate the function at the critical points \\(x = 0\\), \\(x = 1\\), and the vertex of the parabola (if it lies within the interval \\([0, 1]\\)). The vertex of the parabola \\(f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\\) occurs at \\(x = -\\frac{b}{2a}\\) (if \\(a \\neq 0\\)).\n\nLet's consider the values of \\(f(x)\\) at \\(x = 0\\), \\(x = 1\\), and \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) (since the vertex of the parabola can be at \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) if \\(a = -b\\)):\n\n1. \\(f(0) = c\\)\n2. \\(f(1) = a + b + c\\)\n3. \\(f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c\\)\n\nGiven the constraint \\(|f(x)| \\le 1\\) for all \\(0 \\le x \\le 1\\), we have:\n\\[|c| \\le 1\\]\n\\[|a + b + c| \\le 1\\]\n\\[|\\frac{a}{4} + \\frac{b}{2} + c| \\le 1\\]\n\nWe need to maximize \\(|a| + |b| + |c|\\) under these constraints. Let's use Python to find the maximum value by iterating over possible values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) that satisfy these constraints.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the constraints\ndef check_constraints(a, b, c):\n    return (abs(c) <= 1) and (abs(a + b + c) <= 1) and (abs(a/4 + b/2 + c) <= 1)\n\n# Define the function to maximize\ndef objective(a, b, c):\n    return abs(a) + abs(b) + abs(c)\n\n# Initialize the maximum value\nmax_value = 0\nbest_a, best_b, best_c = 0, 0, 0\n\n# Iterate over possible values of a, b, and c\nfor a in np.linspace(-10, 10, 100):\n    for b in np.linspace(-10, 10, 100):\n        for c in np.linspace(-10, 10, 100):\n            if check_constraints(a, b, c):\n                current_value = objective(a, b, c)\n                if current_value > max_value:\n                    max_value = current_value\n                    best_a, best_b, best_c = a, b, c\n\nprint(max_value, best_a, best_b, best_c)\n```\n```output\n18.999999999999993 10.0 10.0 -10.0\n```\nThe output indicates that the maximum value of \\(|a| + |b| + |c|\\) is approxi