ally, we combine the \\(x\\) terms and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe \\(x\\) terms are \\(19x\\) and \\(-4x\\), so we combine them as follows:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x.\\]\n\nThe constant terms are \\(1\\) and \\(-81\\), so we combine them as follows:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80.\\]\n\nPutting these together, the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80.\\]\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the expression as a function to simplify it\r\ndef simplify_expression():\r\n    # Combine the x terms\r\n    x_coefficient = 19 - 4\r\n    # Combine the constant terms\r\n    constant_term = 1 - 81\r\n    # Return the simplified expression\r\n    return f"{x_coefficient}x + {constant_term}"\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = simplify_expression()\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n15x + -80\n```\nThe simplified expression is \\(15x - 80\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15x - 80}\n\\]', "To simplify the given expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. Specifically, we will combine the terms involving \\(x\\) and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe expression is:\n\\[19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\]\n\nFirst, combine the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[19x - 4x = 15x\\]\n\nNext, combine the constant terms:\n\\[1 - 81 = -80\\]\n\nSo, the simplified expression is:\n\\[15x - 80\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpression = 19*x + 1 - 4*x - 81\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expression = simplify(expression)\r\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\n15*x - 80\n```\nThe simplified expression is indeed \\(\\boxed{15x - 80}\\).", "To simplify the expression \\(19x + 1 - 4x - 81\\), we need to combine like terms. Specifically, we combine the terms involving \\(x\\) and the constant terms separately.\n\nThe terms involving \\(x\\) are \\(19