 integers in the solution set are \\(-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\\). \n\nLet's count these integers using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the range of the solution set\nstart = -3.6\nend = 7.6\n\n# Find all integers within this range\ninteger_solution_set = [x for x in range(int(start), int(end) + 1) if start <= x <= end]\n\n# Count the number of integers in the solution set\nnumber_of_integers = len(integer_solution_set)\nprint((number_of_integers, integer_solution_set))\n```\n```output\n(11, [-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\n```\nThe integers in the solution set of \\(|x-2| \\leq 5.6\\) are \\(-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\\). There are 11 integers in this set.\n\nThus, the number of integers in the solution set is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "To solve the inequality \\(|x-2| \\leq 5.6\\), we need to consider the definition of absolute value. The inequality \\(|x-2| \\leq 5.6\\) means that the distance between \\(x\\) and 2 on the number line is less than or equal to 5.6. This translates to:\n\n\\[\n-5.6 \\leq x-2 \\leq 5.6\n\\]\n\nAdding 2 to all parts of the inequality, we get:\n\n\\[\n-5.6 + 2 \\leq x \\leq 5.6 + 2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n-3.6 \\leq x \\leq 7.6\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find how many integers are in the interval \\([-3.6, 7.6]\\). The integers in this range are \\(-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\\). \n\nSo, the number of integers in the solution set is 11. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the range of the inequality\nlower_bound = -3.6\nupper_bound = 7.6\n\n# Find all integers in the range [lower_bound, upper_bound]\nintegers_in_range = [x for x in range(int(lower_bound), int(upper_bound) + 1) if lower_bound <= x <= upper_bound]\n\n# The number of integers in the range\nnumber_of_integers = len(integers_in_range)\nprint((number_of_integers, integers_in_range))\n```\n```output\n(11, [-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7])\n```\nThe integers in the solution set of \\(|x-2| \\leq 5.6\