nd $\\overrightarrow{v_2}$, and therefore perpendicular to the plane. So, the equation of the plane can be written as:\n\n$$-8x + 8y - 16z = d$$\n\n4. To find the value of $d$, we can substitute the coordinates of one of the points into the equation:\n\n$$-8(-3) + 8(-2) - 16(-5) = d$$\n\n$$24 - 16 + 80 = d$$\n\n$$88 = d$$\n\n5. Therefore, the equation of the plane is:\n\n$$-8x + 8y - 16z = 88$$\n\n$$6x + 2y - 4z + 2 = 0$$\n####\nThe answer is $6x + 2y - 4z + 2 = 0$\n\nHuman: Find the eigenvalues of the given matrix, rounded to three decimal places:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n4 & -\\frac{26}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{26}{3} & 8 & \\frac{14}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3} & 9 & -\\frac{14}{3} \\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n\\]\n\nAssistant: The eigenvalues of the matrix are:\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1 = -6.861, \\quad \\lambda_2 = 7.264 - 7.328i, \\quad \\lambda_3 = 7.264 + 7.328i\n\\]\n\nHuman: Calculate the Jacobian matrix of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{F}(x, y, z) = \\begin{pmatrix} f(x, y, z) \\\\ g(x, y, z) \\\\ h(x, y, z) \\end{pmatrix}$, where $f(x, y, z) = \\arccos(x+y)$, $g(x, y, z) = \\arccos(y)$, and $h(x, y, z) = z^{1/3}$.\n\nAssistant: The Jacobian matrix of $\\mathbf{F}(x, y, z)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{F}}{\\partial \\mathbf{x}} = \\left(\n\\begin{array}{ccc}\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+y)^2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+y)^2}} & 0 \\\\\n0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-y^2}} & 0 \\\\\n0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3z^{2/3}} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\right)\n$$\n\nHuman: Find the equation of the plane that passes through the points $P_1\\left(\\frac{5}{2},-4,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$, $P_2\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$, and $P_3\\left(-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.\n\nAssistant: To find the equation of the plane, we first need to determine a normal vector to the plane. We can do this by taking the cross product of two vectors formed by the given points. Let's choose vectors $\\overrightarrow{P_1P_2}$ and $\\overrightarrow{P_1P_3}$.\n\