\\mod 11\\) and \\(8^8 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[8^2 = 64 \\equiv 9 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^4 = (8^2)^2 \\equiv 9^2 \\equiv 81 \\equiv 4 \\mod 11\\]\n\\[8^8 = (8^4)^2 \\equiv 4^2 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 5 \\mod 11\\]\n\nSo:\n\\[8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 8^4 + 8^8 \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\mod 11\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(9\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to compute (base^exponent) % mod using a fast exponentiation method\ndef modular_exponentiation(base, exponent, mod):\n    result = 1\n    base = base % mod\n    while exponent > 0:\n        if (exponent % 2) == 1:  # If exponent is odd, multiply base with result\n            result = (result * base) % mod\n        exponent = exponent >> 1  # Divide the exponent by 2\n        base = (base * base) % mod  # Square the base\n    return result\n\n# Given values\nbase1 = 129\nbase2 = 96\nexponent1 = 34\nexponent2 = 38\nmod = 11\n\n# Simplify bases modulo 11\nbase1_mod = base1 % mod\nbase2_mod = base2 % mod\n\n# Compute the modular exponentiations\nresult1 = modular_exponentiation(base1_mod, exponent1, mod)\nresult2 = modular_exponentiation(base2_mod, exponent2, mod)\n\n# Sum the results and take modulo 11\nfinal_result = (result1 + result2) % mod\nprint(final_result)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To find the remainder when \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) is divided by 11, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can simplify the problem by reducing the bases modulo 11 first.\n\n1. Calculate \\(129 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}\\]\nSo, \\(129^{34} \\equiv 8^{34} \\pmod{11}\\).\n\n2. Calculate \\(96 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}\\]\nSo, \\(96^{38} \\equiv 8^{38} \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nNow we need to find \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\mod 11\\). We can simplify this f