\\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{36} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6^{n-2}} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1) \\cdot 25}{2 \\cdot 36 \\cdot 6^{n-2}} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2 \\cdot 36 \\cdot 6^{n-2}} = \\frac{1}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}} = \\frac{1}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{6^{n-1}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ 3n(n-1) = 6^{n-1} \\]\nNow we need to find the value of $n$ that satisfies this equation. We can try different values of $n$ to find the solution. Let's start with $n=3$ and go upwards.\n- For $n=3$: $3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 = 6^2 \\implies 18 = 36$ (False)\n- For $n=4$: $3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 = 6^3 \\implies 36 = 216$ (False)\n- For $n=5$: $3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 = 6^4 \\implies 60 = 1296$ (False)\n- For $n=6$: $3 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 = 6^5 \\implies 90 = 7776$ (False)\n- For $n=7$: $3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 = 6^6 \\implies 126 = 46656$ (False)\n- For $n=8$: $3 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 = 6^7 \\implies 168 = 279936$ (False)\n- For $n=9$: $3 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 = 6^8 \\implies 216 = 1679616$ (False)\n- For $n=10$: $3 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 9 = 6^9 \\implies 270 = 10077696$ (False)\n- For $n=11$: $3 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 10 = 6^{10} \\implies 330 = 60466176$ (False)\n- For $n=12$: $3 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 11 = 6^{11} \\implies 396 = 362797056$ (False)\n- For $n=13$: $3 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 12 = 6^{12} \\implies 468 = 2176782336$ (False)\n- For $n=14$: $3 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 13 = 6^{13} \\implies 546 = 13060694016$ (False)\n- For $n=15$: $3 \\cdot 15 \\cdot 14 = 6^{14} \\implies 630 = 78364164096$ (False)\n- For $n=16$: $3 \\cdot 16 \\cdot 15 = 6^{15} \\implies 720 = 470184984576$ (False)\n- For $n=17$: $3 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 16 = 6^{16} \\implies 816 = 2821109907456$ (False)\n- For $n=18$: $3 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 17 = 6^{17} \\implies 918 = 16926659444736$ (False)\n- For $n=19$: $3 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 18 = 6^{18} \\implies 1026 = 101559956668416$ (False)\n- For $n=20$: $3 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 19 = 6^{19} \\implies 1140 = 609359740010496$ (False)\n- For $n=21$: $3 \\cdot 21 \\cd