.\n2. The number of boys will then be \\(35 - 13k\\).\n3. According to the problem, the number of girls is greater than the number of boys, so \\(13k > 35 - 13k\\).\n\nWe can solve the inequality \\(13k > 35 - 13k\\) to find the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[13k + 13k > 35\\]\n\\[26k > 35\\]\n\\[k > \\frac{35}{26}\\]\n\\[k > 1.346\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 2. We also need to check that the number of girls is less than or equal to 35, so \\(13k \\leq 35\\). The largest possible value for \\(k\\) is 2 because \\(13 \\times 3 = 39\\) which is greater than 35.\n\nSo, the only possible value for \\(k\\) is 2. Let's calculate the number of girls and boys:\n\n- Number of girls = \\(13 \\times 2 = 26\\)\n- Number of boys = \\(35 - 26 = 9\\)\n\nWe can verify that 26 is a multiple of 13, and 26 > 9, so the conditions are satisfied. Therefore, the number of boys at the meeting is 9.\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this solution.\n```python\n# Total number of students\ntotal_students = 35\n\n# Iterate over possible values of k\nfor k in range(1, total_students // 13 + 1):\n    girls = 13 * k\n    boys = total_students - girls\n    if girls > boys:\n        number_of_boys = boys\n        break\n\nprint(number_of_boys)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of boys at the meeting is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Let \\( G \\) be the number of girls and \\( B \\) be the number of boys.\n2. According to the problem, \\( G + B = 35 \\).\n3. \\( G \\) is a multiple of 13. So, \\( G \\) can be 13, 26, or 39 (since \\( G \\) must be less than 35).\n4. Since there are more girls than boys, \\( G > B \\). This means \\( G > 35 - G \\), or \\( 2G > 35 \\), or \\( G > 17.5 \\). Since \\( G \\) is an integer, \\( G \\geq 18 \\).\n\nFrom the above points, the only possible value for \\( G \\) is 26 (since 13 is less than 18 and 39 is more than 35). So, \\( G = 26 \\). The