the archery class this week is \\(\\boxed{41}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we need to find the number of students \\( n \\) that shows up to the archery class this week, where \\( 30 \\leq n \\leq 50 \\). The key points are:\n\n1. \\( n \\) is not divisible by any integer \\( k \\) (where \\( 2 \\leq k \\leq n-1 \\)).\n2. \\( n + 2 \\) is also not divisible by any integer \\( k \\) (where \\( 2 \\leq k \\leq n \\)).\n\nThis means that \\( n \\) and \\( n + 2 \\) are both prime numbers, because if they had any divisors other than 1 and themselves, the conditions would be violated. Therefore, \\( n \\) and \\( n + 2 \\) form a pair of twin primes.\n\nLet\'s write a Python code to find such a pair of twin primes between 30 and 50.\n```python\ndef is_prime(num):\n    """Check if a number is prime."""\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num <= 3:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0 or num % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0 or num % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n\n# Find the twin primes between 30 and 50\nfor n in range(30, 51):\n    if is_prime(n) and is_prime(n + 2):\n        result = n\n        break\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n41\n```\nThe number of students that showed up to the archery class this week is \\(\\boxed{41}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to find a number of students \\( n \\) between 30 and 50 such that \\( n \\) is not divisible by any number from 2 to \\( n-1 \\) (i.e., \\( n \\) is a prime number). Additionally, \\( n + 2 \\) (since Betty and Wilma join the students) should also not be divisible by any number from 2 to \\( n+1 \\), meaning \\( n + 2 \\) should also be a prime number. In other words, we need to find a pair of prime numbers that are two units apart, also known as twin primes, within the range from 30 to 52.\n\nLet's write Python code to find such a pair of prime numbers.\n\n```python\ndef is_prime(num):\n