2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's find the sum of the first row:\n\\[\n(n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = n-3 + 3 + n+1 = 2n + 1\n\\]\nSo, the common sum \\( S \\) is \\( 2n + 1 \\).\n\nNext, let's find the sum of the second row:\n\\[\n(n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = n+2 + 2n-9 + 1 = 3n - 6\n\\]\nSince this sum must also be equal to \\( S \\), we have:\n\\[\n3n - 6 = 2n + 1\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[\n3n - 2n = 1 + 6 \\implies n = 7\n\\]\n\nTo verify, let's substitute \\( n = 7 \\) into the magic square and check the sums:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n4 & 3 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n9 & 5 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 7 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe sum of each row, each column, and the two main diagonals should be \\( 2(7) + 1 = 15 \\).\n\nLet's check the rows:\n1. \\( 4 + 3 + 8 = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 9 + 5 + 1 = 15 \\)\n3. \\( 2 + 7 + 6 = 15 \\)\n\nLet's check the columns:\n1. \\( 4 + 9 + 2 = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 3 + 5 + 7 = 15 \\)\n3. \\( 8 + 1 + 6 = 15 \\)\n\nLet's check the diagonals:\n1. \\( 4 + 5 + 6 = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 8 + 5 + 2 = 15 \\)\n\nSince all the sums are equal to 15, the value of \\( n \\) is indeed correct. Therefore, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to use the property of a magic square, which states that the sum of the numbers in each row, each column, and each of the two main diagonals is the same. Let's denote this common sum by \\(S\\).\n\nGiven the magic square:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can write the following equations for the sum of each row, each column, and each diagonal:\n\n1. Sum of the first row: \\((n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = S\\)\n2. Sum of the second row: \\((n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = S\\)\n3. Sum of the third row: \\(2 + n + (n-1) = S\\)\n4. Sum of the first column: \\((n-3) + (n+2) + 2 = S\\)\n5. Sum of the second column: \\(3 + (2n-9) + n = S\\)\n6. Sum of the third column: \\((n+1) + 1 + (n-1) = S\\)\n7. Sum of the main diagonal: \\((n-3) + (2n-9) + (n-1) = S\\)\n8. Sum of the other diagonal: \\((n+1) + (2n-9) + 2 = S\\)\n\nLet's use these equations to find the value of \\(n\\).\n\nFirst, we can simplify the sum of the first row:\n\\[ (n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = 2n + 1 = S \\]\n\nNext, we can simplify the sum of the second row:\n\\[ (n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = 3n - 6 = S \\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(S\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[ 2n + 1 = 3n - 6 \\]\n\\[ 1 + 6 = 3n - 2n \\]\n\\[ 7 = n \\]\n\nSo the value of \\(n\\) is \\(7\\). Let's verify this by substituting \\(n = 7\\) into the magic square and checking that all the sums are equal.\n\nThe magic square with \\(n = 7\\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n4 & 3 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n9 & 5 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & 7 & 6 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can check the sums:\n1. Sum of the first row: \\(4 + 3 + 8 = 15\\)\n2. Sum of the second row: \\(9 + 5 + 1 = 15\\)\n3. Sum of the third row: \\(2 + 7 + 6 = 15\\)\n4. Sum of the first column: \\(4 + 9 + 2 = 15\\)\n5. Sum of the second column: \\(3 + 5 + 7 = 15\\)\n6. Sum of the third column: \\(8 + 1 + 6 = 15\\)\n7. Sum of the main diagonal: \\(4 + 5 + 6 = 15\\)\n8. Sum of the other diagonal: \\(8 + 5 + 2 = 15\\)\n\nSince all the sums are equal to 15, the value of \\(n\\) is indeed correct. Therefore, the value of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\( n \\) that makes the given grid a magic square. In a magic square, the sum of the numbers in each row, each column, and the two main diagonals must be the same. Let's denote this common sum by \\( S \\).\n\nGiven the grid:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nn-3 & 3 & n+1 \\\\\n\\hline\nn+2 & 2n-9 & 1 \\\\\n\\hline\n2 & n & n-1 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nWe can start by calculating the sum of the first row:\n\\[ S = (n-3) + 3 + (n+1) = 2n - 1. \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum of the second row:\n\\[ S = (n+2) + (2n-9) + 1 = 3n - 6. \\]\n\nSince these sums must be equal, we can set them equal to each other and solve for \\( n \\):\n\\[ 2n - 1 = 3n - 6. \\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[ -1 = n - 6, \\]\n\\[ n = 5. \\]\n\nNow, let's verify that \\( n = 5 \\) makes the grid a magic square by calculating the sum of the columns and the diagonals.\n\nThe first column is:\n\\[ (n-3) + (n+2) + 2 = (5-3) + (5+2) + 2 = 2 + 7 + 2 = 11. \\]\nThe second column is:\n\\[ 3 + (2n-9) + n = 3 + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 5 = 3 + 1 + 5 = 9. \\]\nThe third column is:\n\\[ (n+1) + 1 + (n-1) = (5+1) + 1 + (5-1) = 6 + 1 + 4 = 11. \\]\n\nThe main diagonal is:\n\\[ (n-3) + (2n-9) + (n-1) = (5-3) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + (5-1) = 2 + 1 + 4 = 7. \\]\nThe other diagonal is:\n\\[ (n+1) + (2n-9) + 2 = (5+1) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 2 = 6 + 1 + 2 = 9. \\]\n\nIt seems there was an error. Let's recheck the calculations. We need to ensure that all rows, columns, and diagonals sum to the same value. Let's recompute using \\( n = 5 \\):\n\nThe first row is:\n\\[ (5-3) + 3 + (5+1) = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11. \\]\nThe second row is:\n\\[ (5+2) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 1 = 7 + 1 + 1 = 9. \\]\nThe third row is:\n\\[ 2 + 5 + (5-1) = 2 + 5 + 4 = 11. \\]\n\nThe first column is:\n\\[ (5-3) + (5+2) + 2 = 2 + 7 + 2 = 11. \\]\nThe second column is:\n\\[ 3 + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 5 = 3 + 1 + 5 = 9. \\]\nThe third column is:\n\\[ (5+1) + 1 + (5-1) = 6 + 1 + 4 = 11. \\]\n\nThe main diagonal is:\n\\[ (5-3) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + (5-1) = 2 + 1 + 4 = 7. \\]\nThe other diagonal is:\n\\[ (5+1) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 2 = 6 + 1 + 2 = 9. \\]\n\nIt seems there was an error in the initial assumption. Let's recheck the calculations more carefully. We need to ensure that all rows, columns, and diagonals sum to the same value. Let's recompute using \\( n = 5 \\):\n\nThe first row is:\n\\[ (5-3) + 3 + (5+1) = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11. \\]\nThe second row is:\n\\[ (5+2) + (2 \\cdot 5 - 9) + 1 = 7 + 1 + 1 = 9. \\]\nThe third row is:\n\\[ 2 + 