\ln{(2^3)} = 3\\ln{2}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{2}} = \\frac{3\\ln{2}}{\\ln{2}} = 3\n\\]\n\nSo the value of the expression is \\(3\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate each logarithm using natural logarithm (base e)\nlog_2_3 = math.log(3) / math.log(2)\nlog_3_4 = math.log(4) / math.log(3)\nlog_4_5 = math.log(5) / math.log(4)\nlog_5_6 = math.log(6) / math.log(5)\nlog_6_7 = math.log(7) / math.log(6)\nlog_7_8 = math.log(8) / math.log(7)\n\n# Calculate the product of all these logarithms\nresult = log_2_3 * log_3_4 * log_4_5 * log_5_6 * log_6_7 * log_7_8\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n3.0\n```\nThe value of the expression \\(\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To find the value of \\(\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8}\\), we can use the change of base formula for logarithms. The change of base formula states that \\(\\log_a{b} = \\frac{\\log_c{b}}{\\log_c{a}}\\) for any positive base \\(c\\). If we use the natural logarithm (base \\(e\\)), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} = \\frac{\\ln{3}}{\\ln{2}}, \\quad \\log_3{4} = \\frac{\\ln{4}}{\\ln{3}}, \\quad \\log_4{5} = \\frac{\\ln{5}}{\\ln{4}}, \\quad \\log_5{6} = \\frac{\\ln{6}}{\\ln{5}}, \\quad \\log_6{7} = \\frac{\\ln{7}}{\\ln{6}}, \\quad \\log_7{8} = \\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{7}}\n\\]\n\nWhen we multiply these together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8} = \\frac{\\ln{3}}{\\ln{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{4}}{\\ln{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{5}}{\\ln{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{6}}{\\ln{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{7}}{\\ln{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{7}}\n\\]\n\nNotice that this is a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln{3}}{\\ln{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{4}}{\\ln{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{5}}{\\ln{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{6}}{\\l