} - p\\right) p + \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\]\n\\[ = 2p \\left(\\frac{1}{6} - p\\right) + 4 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{2p}{6} - 2p^2 + \\frac{4}{36} \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{p}{3} - 2p^2 + \\frac{1}{9} \\]\n\nWe are given that this probability is \\( \\frac{47}{288} \\). So we can set up the equation:\n\\[ \\frac{p}{3} - 2p^2 + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288} \\]\nMultiplying through by 288 to clear the denominators, we get:\n\\[ 96p - 576p^2 + 32 = 47 \\]\n\\[ 576p^2 - 96p + 15 = 0 \\]\nDividing through by 3, we get:\n\\[ 192p^2 - 32p + 5 = 0 \\]\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( p = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 192 \\), \\( b = -32 \\), and \\( c = 5 \\):\n\\[ p = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{32^2 - 4 \\cdot 192 \\cdot 5}}{2 \\cdot 192} \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{1024 - 3840}}{384} \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{-2816}}{384} \\]\nSince the discriminant is negative, we need to check the possible rational roots of the quadratic equation. The possible rational roots are the factors of the constant term (5) divided by the factors of the leading coefficient (192). The factors of 5 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 5 \\), and the factors of 192 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 4, \\pm 6, \\pm 8, \\pm 12, \\pm 16, \\pm 24, \\pm 32, \\pm 48, \\pm 64, \\pm 96, \\pm 192 \\). The possible rational roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\pm \\frac{1}{12}, \\pm \\frac{1}{16}, \\pm \\frac{1}{24}, \\pm \\frac{1}{32}, \\pm \\frac{1}{48}, \\pm \\frac{1}{64}, \\pm \\frac{1}{96}, \\pm \\frac{1}{192}, \\pm 5, \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, \\pm \\frac{5}{4}, \\pm \\frac{5}{6}, \\pm \\frac{5}{8}, \\pm \\frac{5}{12}, \\pm \\frac{5}{16}, \\pm \\frac{5}{24}, \\pm \\frac{5}{32}, \\pm \\frac{5}{48}, \\pm \\frac{5}{64}, 