 15, which occurs at \\( x = 15 \\).\n\nThus, the minimum value taken by \\( f(x) \\) for \\( p \\leq x \\leq 15 \\) is \\( \\boxed{15} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the function f(x)\ndef f(x, p):\n    return abs(x - p) + abs(x - 15) + abs(x - p - 15)\n\n# Define the interval and the range of p\np_values = [p for p in range(1, 15)]\nmin_values = []\n\n# Calculate the minimum value of f(x) for each p in the interval p <= x <= 15\nfor p in p_values:\n    min_value = min(f(p, p), f(15, p))\n    min_values.append(min_value)\n\n# The minimum value of f(x) for all p in the interval\nmin_f_x = min(min_values)\nprint(min_f_x)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to find the minimum value of the function \\( f(x) = |x - p| + |x - 15| + |x - p - 15| \\) for \\( p \\leq x \\leq 15 \\). Here's the step-by-step approach:\n\n1. **Understand the Function:**\n   The function \\( f(x) \\) is a sum of absolute values. The critical points where the behavior of the function changes are at \\( x = p \\), \\( x = 15 \\), and \\( x = p + 15 \\). However, since we are only considering the interval \\( p \\leq x \\leq 15 \\), we only need to consider the points \\( x = p \\) and \\( x = 15 \\).\n\n2. **Evaluate the Function at the Critical Points:**\n   We need to evaluate \\( f(x) \\) at \\( x = p \\) and \\( x = 15 \\) to find the minimum value.\n\n   - At \\( x = p \\):\n     \\[\n     f(p) = |p - p| + |p - 15| + |p - p - 15| = 0 + (15 - p) + 15 = 30 - p\n     \\]\n\n   - At \\( x = 15 \\):\n     \\[\n     f(15) = |15 - p| + |15 - 15| + |15 - p - 15| = (15 - p) + 0 + | - p| = 15 - p + p = 15\n     \\]\n\n3. **Compare the Values:**\n   Since \\( 0 < p < 15 \\), it is clear that \\( 30 - p > 15 \\). Therefore, the minimum value of \\( f(x) \\) in the interval \\( p \\leq x \\leq 15 \\) is 15, which occurs at \\( x = 15 \\).\n\nThus, the minimum value taken by \\( f(x) \\) for \\( p \\leq x \\leq 15 \\) is \\(\\boxed{15}