or -33\n    # The smallest possible sum of distinct integers whose product is 33 or -33 is 0\n    roots = [1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11]\n    sum_of_roots = sum(roots)\n    a_n_minus_1 = -2 * sum_of_roots\n    return abs(a_n_minus_1)\n\n# Calculate the least possible value of |a_{n-1}|\nleast_value = least_possible_value()\nprint(least_value)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nThe least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "Given the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with distinct integer roots, we need to find the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of the polynomial (with the leading coefficient factored out) is given by \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}\\). If the roots are \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\), then:\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2} \\]\nor\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n). \\]\n\nWe need to find distinct integers whose product is 66 (since the product of the roots, with the leading coefficient factored out, is \\((-1)^n \\frac{a_0}{2} = \\frac{66}{2} = 33\\)) and whose sum, when multiplied by -2, gives the smallest possible absolute value for \\( a_{n-1} \\).\n\nThe integer factorizations of 66 are:\n\\[ 66 = 1 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-66), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 1 \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-33), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 1 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot (-22), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 1 \\cdot (-6) \\cdot (-11), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-33), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 2 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot (-11), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 3 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-22), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 3 \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-11), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-11), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-3), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 11, \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-11), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 11, \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-3), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3, \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-2), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2, \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 11 \\cdot (-1), \\]\n\\[ 66 = 6 \\cdot 11 \\cdo