_least_a_n_minus_1():\n    # Possible factorizations of 66\n    factorizations = [\n        [1, -1, -66], [1, -2, -33], [1, -3, -22], [1, -6, -11],\n        [2, -1, -33], [2, -3, -11], [3, -1, -22], [3, -2, -11],\n        [6, -1, -11], [6, -2, -3], [6, 1, -11], [6, 1, 11],\n        [6, -1, 11], [6, 2, -3], [6, 2, 3], [6, 3, -2],\n        [6, 3, 2], [6, 11, -1], [6, 11, 1], [11, -1, 6],\n        [11, 1, 6], [11, -2, 3], [11, 2, 3], [11, -3, 2],\n        [11, 3, 2], [11, 6, -1], [11, 6, 1], [22, -1, 3],\n        [22, 1, 3], [33, -1, 2], [33, 1, 2], [66, -1, 1],\n        [66, 1, -1]\n    ]\n    \n    min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = float('inf')\n    \n    for factors in factorizations:\n        # Calculate the sum of the roots\n        sum_of_roots = sum(factors)\n        # Calculate the value of |a_{n-1}|\n        abs_a_n_minus_1 = abs(2 * sum_of_roots)\n        # Update the minimum value if the current one is smaller\n        if abs_a_n_minus_1 < min_abs_a_n_minus_1:\n            min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = abs_a_n_minus_1\n    \n    return min_abs_a_n_minus_1\n\n# Find the least possible value of |a_{n-1}|\nleast_abs_a_n_minus_1 = find_least_a_n_minus_1()\nprint(least_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n2\n```\nThe least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with integer coefficients and integer roots. Given that the polynomial \\(f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66\\) has distinct integer roots, we can use Vieta's formulas to relate the coefficients of the polynomial to the roots.\n\nLet's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots taken one at a time (with the polynomial's leading coefficient factored out) is given by \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}\\). Since \\(a_n = 2\\), we have:\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}. \\]\nThis means:\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n). \\]\nTo minimize \\(|a_{n-1}|\\), we need to find distinct integer roots \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\) such that the sum \\(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n\\) is as close to zero as possible.\n\nAdditionally, the product of the roots (with the polynomial's leading coefficient factored out) is given by \\((-1)^n \\frac{a_0}{a_n}\\). Since \\(a_0 = 66\\) and \\(a_n = 2\\), we have:\n\\[ r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = (-1)^n \\frac{66}{2} = (-1)^n \\cdot 33. \\]\nThis means the product of the roots is either \\(33\\) or \\(-33\\).\n\nLet's find the distinct integer roots that satisfy these conditions. We need to find distinct integers whose product is \\(33\\) or \\(-33\\) and whose sum is as close to zero as possible.\n\nThe factorizations of \\(33\\) are:\n\\[ 33 = 33 \\times 1 \\times (-1) \\times (-1) \\]\n\\[ 33 = 11 \\times 3 \\times 1 \\times (-1) \\]\n\\[ 33 = 33 \\times (-1) \\]\n\\[ 33 = 11 \\times 3 \\]\n\\[ 33 = 33 \\]\n\nThe factorizations of \\(-33\\) are similar, but with appropriate signs.\n\nWe need to check the sums of these factors to find the combination that gives the smallest absolute value for \\(a_{n-1}\\).\n\nLet's check the possible combinations using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\n# Possible factorizations of 33 and -33\nfactorizations = [\n    (33, 1, -1, -1),\n    (11, 3, 1, -1),\n    (33, -1),\n    (11, 3),\n    (33),\n    (-33, 1, 1, 1),\n    (-11, -3, 1, 1),\n    (-33, 1),\n    (-11, -3),\n    (-33)\n]\n\n# Function to find the minimum |a_{n-1}|\ndef find_min_abs_a_n_minus_1(factorizations):\n    min_abs_a_n_minus_1 = float('inf')\n    best_combination = None\n    \n    for factors in factorizations:\n        # Generate all unique combinations of factors\n        for r in range(1, len(factors) + 1):\n            for combo in combinations(factors, r):\n                product = 1\n                for factor in combo:\n                    product *= factor\n                if product == 33 or product ==